{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":78544,"name":"Petr Viktorin","username":"encukou","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/encukou/{size}/2461_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-19T16:38:22.314Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eHello,\u003cbr\u003e\nI’d like to propose the following changes to our backwards compatibility policy. I see them as clarifications and/or codifying the status quo, but, you might disagree.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThank you to other members of the 2022 Steering Council for lots of discussion on this. The remaining \u003cem\u003ebad\u003c/em\u003e ideas are mine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI sent this as a \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/python/peps/pull/2931\"\u003edraft PR\u003c/a\u003e, see there for the current version.\u003cbr\u003e\nThe initial proposal, with more rationale, is below:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-78544-explicit-policy-for-undocumented-things-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-78544-explicit-policy-for-undocumented-things-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eExplicit policy for undocumented things\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"diff\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-diff\"\u003e - Anything documented publicly as being private.\n+  Note that if something is not documented at all, it is *not*\n+  automatically considered private.\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOur documentation doesn’t cover everything. When it’s lacking, people reach for the source code or other sources, from StackOverflow to blogs. I’m all for better stability expectation management, but for existing stuff (which might be decades old), by default we should assume that someone uses it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-78544-examples-for-extended-deprecation-period-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-78544-examples-for-extended-deprecation-period-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eExamples for extended deprecation period\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"diff\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-diff\"\u003e-   It's fine to wait more than two releases.\n+\n+   It's fine to wait more than two releases, for example:\n+\n+   - If the expected maintenance overhead and security risk of the\n+     deprecated behavior is small (e.g. an old function is reimplemented\n+     in terms of a new, more general one), it can stay indefinitely\n+     (or until the situation changes).\n+\n+   - If the deprecated feature is replaced by a new one, it should\n+     generally be removed only after the last Python version\n+     *without* the new feature reaches end of support.\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt was too easy to misread the PEP as saying that Python deprecation periods are “two releases”, or “two releases by default”. Rather, I see the two releases as the \u003cem\u003eminimum\u003c/em\u003e, a line that needs a project-wide decision to cross. (The project-wide decision is implemented/approximated as a SC ruling.)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-78544-py_deprecated-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-78544-py_deprecated-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003ePy_DEPRECATED\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"diff\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-diff\"\u003e-   [...] Compiler warnings are also acceptable. [...]\n+   For C API, a compiler warning generated by the ``Py_DEPRECATED`` macro\n+   is also acceptable.\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ePy_DEPRECATED\u003c/code\u003e macro doesn’t contain the info a deprecation is supposed to have (“the release the incompatibility is expected to become the default and a link to an issue that users can post feedback to.”) It also relies on compiler extensions, so users with less common compilers won’t see it.\u003cbr\u003e\nIdeally that should all be fixed in \u003ccode\u003ePy_DEPRECATED\u003c/code\u003e some day, but in the meantime, let’s explicitly say that \u003ccode\u003ePy_DEPRECATED\u003c/code\u003e is fine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-78544-clarifications-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-78544-clarifications-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eClarifications\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"diff\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-diff\"\u003e-2. Add a warning. [...)\n+2. Add a warning to the current ``main`` branch. [...]\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"diff\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-diff\"\u003e-  [...] (e.g. for a warning in Python 3.10, you either wait\n+  [...] (e.g. for a warning in Python 3.10.0, you either wait\n    until at least Python 3.12 or Python 4.0 to make the change).\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGenerally, adding a deprecation warning is not a bugfix so it shouldn’t go in a stable release. These changes should nudge people to not worry about older branches.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-78544-changelog-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-78544-changelog-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eChangelog\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe PEP should also get a Changelog section with a link to this discussion. That’s not in the PR yet.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-20T00:40:02.152Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":762,"reads":161,"readers_count":160,"score":3967.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Petr Viktorin","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://github.com/python/peps/pull/2931","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 387 (Backwards Compatibility Policy) updates/clarifications by encukou · Pull Request #2931 · python/peps · GitHub","clicks":57}],"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":8}],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":26,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":8}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":8,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":78598,"name":"Barry Warsaw","username":"barry","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/{size}/42_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-19T23:33:58.021Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"encukou\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/encukou/48/2461_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Petr Viktorin:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003efor existing stuff (which might be decades old), by default we should assume that someone uses it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSomeone probably does, but I’m not sure if that should extend the deprecation policy to undocumented APIs.  How would I then document that an API \u003cem\u003eis\u003c/em\u003e private or should be considered unstable?  Do I have to do that with every method and variable in my code (probably at least narrowing out leading-underscore names)?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-19T23:33:58.021Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":136,"readers_count":135,"score":72.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Barry Warsaw","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"steering-council","flair_url":"fab-python","flair_bg_color":"FFE873","flair_color":"4B8BBE","flair_group_id":45,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"Steering Council Member","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":17,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/2","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":78610,"name":"Brett Cannon","username":"brettcannon","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/brettcannon/{size}/34895_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-20T00:45:32.868Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"barry\" data-post=\"2\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/48/42_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Barry Warsaw:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow would I then document that an API \u003cem\u003eis\u003c/em\u003e private or should be considered unstable?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eProbably leading underscore or \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0689/\" class=\"inline-onebox\"\u003ePEP 689 – Unstable C API tier | peps.python.org\u003c/a\u003e .\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-20T00:45:32.868Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":134,"readers_count":133,"score":71.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Brett Cannon","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"packaging-council","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/0/5/05b580b0d5d0ba0258ddcb3685ca7006fcd0f01b.png","flair_bg_color":"","flair_color":"","flair_group_id":48,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0689/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 689 – Unstable C API tier | peps.python.org","clicks":17}],"read":true,"user_title":"Packaging Council Member","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":3}],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":36,"hidden":false,"trust_level":4,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/3","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":3,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":78628,"name":"Petr Viktorin","username":"encukou","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/encukou/{size}/2461_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-20T08:48:32.968Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eRight, use a leading underscore. That’s the first thing on the PEP’s list of what’s private.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-20T08:48:32.968Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":129,"readers_count":128,"score":60.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Petr Viktorin","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":26,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/4","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":78663,"name":"Barry Warsaw","username":"barry","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/{size}/42_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-20T19:39:20.104Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"encukou\" data-post=\"4\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/encukou/48/2461_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Petr Viktorin:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003euse a leading underscore\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSure, of course I get that, and of course do the same in my own code.  But what about existing stdlib code that this PEP covers?  Do we go a-rewriting to ensure that stdlib APIs conform to this convention, otherwise they are now all considered public?  And if we do, then doesn’t that effectively break the backward compatibility constraints proposed in the PEP’s revision?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-20T19:39:20.104Z","reply_count":2,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":123,"readers_count":122,"score":49.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Barry Warsaw","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"steering-council","flair_url":"fab-python","flair_bg_color":"FFE873","flair_color":"4B8BBE","flair_group_id":45,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"Steering Council Member","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":17,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":78667,"name":"Brett Cannon","username":"brettcannon","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/brettcannon/{size}/34895_2.png","created_at":"2022-12-20T20:08:36.706Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"barry\" data-post=\"5\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/48/42_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Barry Warsaw:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDo we go a-rewriting to ensure that stdlib APIs conform to this convention, otherwise they are now all considered public?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAssuming that module has actual stewards who care, you probably could.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"barry\" data-post=\"5\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/48/42_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Barry Warsaw:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd if we do, then doesn’t that effectively break the backward compatibility constraints proposed in the PEP’s revision?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOnly if you don’t follow the deprecation policy for the old names. \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/wink.png?v=12\" title=\":wink:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":wink:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e You can probably do a redirection where the old name raises the warning.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2022-12-20T20:08:36.706Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":121,"readers_count":120,"score":69.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Brett Cannon","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"packaging-council","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/0/5/05b580b0d5d0ba0258ddcb3685ca7006fcd0f01b.png","flair_bg_color":"","flair_color":"","flair_group_id":48,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"Packaging Council Member","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":3}],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":36,"hidden":false,"trust_level":4,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/6","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":3,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":79708,"name":"Alyssa Coghlan","username":"ncoghlan","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/ncoghlan/{size}/14266_2.png","created_at":"2023-01-01T02:55:04.941Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"barry\" data-post=\"5\" data-topic=\"22042\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/barry/48/42_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Barry Warsaw:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDo we go a-rewriting to ensure that stdlib APIs conform to this convention, otherwise they are now all considered public?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt wouldn’t make sense to do that pre-emptively, but if a future refactoring wanted to make changes to that “intended to be internal but not marked as such” API, \u003cem\u003ethat\u003c/em\u003e would be the time to add the missing underscore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe old unintentionally public API would gain a code comment explaining why it still existed as a compatibility wrapper around the new clearly private API. Whether it was actively deprecated or not would depend on the specific situation.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2023-01-01T02:55:04.941Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":4,"reads":99,"readers_count":98,"score":39.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":22042,"topic_slug":"pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications","display_username":"Alyssa Coghlan","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":98,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-387-backwards-compatibility-policy-updates-clarifications/22042/7","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":101313,"name":"Petr Viktorin","username":"encukou","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/encukou/{size}/2461_2.png","created_at":"2023-06-19T09:44:49.89